<p style="text-align: center;"><img src="/images/2016/North-Bluff.jpg" alt="North Bluff. 5x7, oil on linen panel. 2016" width="550" height="390" /></p>
<p style="text-align: center;">North Bluff. 5x7, oil on linen panel. 2016</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Today I took a bit of a different turn in my sketch-a-thon. Because I had other things that I needed to get done, I was only able to get a couple of sketches finished.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">I revisited some of the scraped-off field studies that I did yesterday, working from a combination of memory and the notan sketches I had done on site.</p>
<p style="text-align: left;">Things didn't go so well at first which turned out to be a stroke of luck:&nbsp;<span style="line-height: 1.42857;">I ended up pushing the foreground paint around with a painting knife and losing all the detail that I'd tried to paint. When I made a loose smear of a blue gray across the top of that I felt like I finally had something.</span></p>
<p style="text-align: left;"><span style="line-height: 1.42857;">This is exactly the kind of thing that I wanted to get out of doing so much sketching - a freeing up and a way to look differently at something I've done many times in the past. This is only a small painting but, in a way, it represents a big step forward for me.</span></p>